Batching reaches the device floor from inside IRON -- and IRON's own per-call host work
eats almost all of it
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), native Windows, mlir-aie ironenv + XRT SDK.
Took a hardware context. Witnesses below: no foreign context on the device,
HOST_LOAD_VERDICT CLEAR.

New code, both in this repository, nothing outside it modified:
  kernels/dispatch_floor/iron_batch.py          the batching context manager
  kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_iron_batch.py  this measurement

Commands (repo root, ironenv, XRT SDK on PATH and pyxrt on PYTHONPATH):
  python kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_iron_batch.py
  python kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_iron_batch.py --design groupnorm --L 150528 \
      --batch-sizes 1,4,16,64 --reps 2

WHY. results/aie/dispatch_runlist_npu.log amortised a dispatch to 36.3 us against IRON's
617.0 us -- 17x -- by driving raw pyxrt against a cached xclbin. That log closed with the
limit stated plainly: "Nothing here is an IRON result. The batched path is raw pyxrt; IRON's
@iron.jit does not use runlists at all, and nothing under aie/utils/hostruntime/ references
one. Reaching 36 us from a real design needs that host path written, which this run does not
do." This run writes that host path and measures it. It also answers the other open caveat --
"this is a no-compute passthrough: a real kernel's own configuration cost lands inside the
dispatch and pushes its floor above this one" -- by running a real kernel through it.

HOW IT HOOKS IN, WITHOUT TOUCHING THE SHARED TOOLCHAIN. XRTHostRuntime.run() validates
arguments against the xclbin ABI, prepares the instruction buffer, then submits with
`kernel_handle.kernel(3, insts_bo, insts_bytes, *buffers)` followed by `.wait()`. In pyxrt
`kernel(...)` creates AND STARTS a run, which is exactly what cannot go into a runlist. So the
context manager swaps `kernel_handle.kernel` for a proxy whose call builds the run UNSTARTED
-- `pyxrt.run(kernel)` plus `set_arg` -- adds it to a runlist, and hands back a deferred
stand-in. Every other step of IRON's run() executes unchanged: the same ABI validation, the
same instruction buffer, the same argument order. The patch is installed and removed by the
context manager. ~/mlir-aie is not edited.

    with iron_batch.batched(max_in_flight=64):
        for i in range(64):
            design(a[i], b[i], c[i])     # queued, not executed
    # one execute(), one wait(), on leaving the block

TWO THINGS THE HARNESS HAS TO GET RIGHT, BECAUSE BOTH FAIL SILENTLY.
  1. Buffers must not be shared between queued calls. Concurrent buffer access within a
     runlist is undefined and the symptom is wrong data, not an error. Every queued call's
     buffers are checked against the rest of the batch and a repeat raises; the guard was
     tested and does fire.
  2. Batching gives up per-call completion status ENTIRELY. IRON compares each call's wait()
     against ERT_CMD_STATE_COMPLETED, and at queue time that answer does not exist. It cannot
     be recovered afterwards either: a run inside a runlist cannot be polled on this binding
     -- run.state() raises "Cannot poll a command that has not been submitted", because the
     runlist owns submission and runlist.wait() is the only completion signal there is. So
     VERIFYING OUTPUT BUFFERS IS THE ONLY CORRECTNESS GATE UNDER BATCHING. Every row below is
     gated on it, and a batch that silently did nothing would otherwise look extremely fast.

TWO CLOCKS, ANSWERING DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.
  runlist us/run   perf_counter around runlist.execute() + wait(), divided by the batch size.
                   The DEVICE cost per dispatch. This is the column comparable to 36.3 us.
  wall/call us     all time in the block divided by the number of calls. Includes everything
                   IRON does per call before the submit. This is what a caller pays.
  host share       the difference. IRON's own per-call host work.

PRE-RUN WITNESSES
Two samples, bracketing the captured series. PRE-RUN read busy_cores=1.22, no python
process in the top list, HOST_LOAD_VERDICT CLEAR, no hardware contexts on the device.
POST-RUN is printed in full below: still CLEAR and still no foreign context, but a
python process not present beforehand now holds about a core. It is disclosed rather
than hidden; the two passthrough series bracket the runs and agree to 1% on the
runlist column, so the result is not sensitive to it.

