--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE DEVICE HALF WORKS, AND IT REPRODUCES THE RAW-PYXRT NUMBER FROM INSIDE IRON. On the
passthrough the runlist bracket falls monotonically with batch size in both series -- 167.3
and 170.7 us at N=1, 45.7 and 45.5 at N=16, 37.9 and 37.5 at N=64. The raw-pyxrt harness
measured 36.3 us on the same design and payload. So the 17x is real, it survives going
through IRON's argument handling rather than around it, and iron_batch.py is how an ordinary
@iron.jit design reaches it. The caveat "nothing here is an IRON result" is closed.

THE REAL KERNEL CLOSES THE OTHER CAVEAT, AND CONFIRMS THE READING FROM THE OTHER SIDE.
GroupNorm's batched device cost falls to 838.3, 823.8 and 829.8 us per dispatch at N=4, 16
and 64 and then stops. That plateau is not a floor this measurement imposed -- it is the
kernel's own compute. results/aie/groupnorm_bf16_kernel_npu.log measured this same design at
this same L independently and reports 835.8 us per call on the NPU (min 807.7), against
1899.3 us on the CPU. All three batched figures land on that number to within 1.5%. Batching
removed about 140 us of device-side dispatch overhead (967.5 us at N=1) and left the work
itself. So a real kernel's dispatch cost is the same order as the passthrough's; its
configuration cost does NOT swamp it. Treat 36-38 us as a floor, as that log said -- but a
floor that a real 8-core bf16 kernel sits only its own compute above, not orders above.

AND THE END-TO-END GAIN IS 1.2x TO 1.4x, NOT 17x. That is the half that matters to a caller.
Against each series' own unbatched median, wall time per call improves 1.26x (676.2 -> 537.1)
and 1.37x (729.0 -> 530.9) on the passthrough, and 1.23x (1844.5 -> 1495.7) on GroupNorm. The
reason is in the host-share column, and it is FLAT IN BATCH SIZE: 477.9 to 528.5 us on the
passthrough across every N >= 4 in both series, 665.8 to 780.8 us on GroupNorm. Batching does
not touch it, because it is not dispatch -- it is what IRON does per call before the submit:
argument validation against the xclbin ABI, buffer preparation, instruction-buffer setup.
GroupNorm's larger host share is consistent with its much larger buffers.

SO THE 447 us HOST TERM IS REPRODUCED, AND LOCALISED. dispatch_floor_npu.log split the 617.0
us floor into a 169.8 us hardware bracket and 447.3 us of host-side cost, and said that cost
"sits outside the hardware bracket". This run measures the same term from a different
direction -- 478-529 us on the same design, at every batch size -- and shows it is
independent of how the submit is done. Batching was the fix for the dispatch half of the
floor. The host half needs a different fix, and it is now the larger of the two by more than
an order of magnitude: 37.5 us of device against ~500 us of host.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE GO/NO-GO RULE, AND IT IS A CORRECTION. dispatch_runlist_npu.log wrote
that batching moves the rule from "must exceed 617 us" to "must exceed about 36 us, a factor
of 17", and this repo's docs repeated it. That is true only of a RAW-PYXRT driver that gives
up IRON's argument handling. It is NOT true through IRON. There are three thresholds, not
two:

    unbatched @iron.jit call        617.0 us   published; 676-729 us this sitting
    batched @iron.jit call       ~531-537 us   this log
    batched raw-pyxrt driver         36.3 us   dispatch_runlist_npu.log

The four verdicts that log reopened -- MobileNetV2 whole model, MobileViT stage-2 attention,
attention stage 2, GroupNorm at L <= 18816 -- reopen at 36 us only for a caller willing to
write a raw-pyxrt driver. Through IRON they still face a ~531 us threshold, which is 14%
below the published 617.0 us and 21-27% below this sitting's own unbatched medians -- not
17x below either of them. Of that list only MobileNetV2's whole model
(1720 us CPU) clears 531 us; the other three do not.

WHY N=1 AND N=2 ARE SLOWER THAN UNBATCHED, WHICH IS NOT NOISE. A one-call "batch" pays for
constructing a runlist and gets nothing amortised, so it is strictly worse: 0.91x and 0.86x
on the passthrough, 0.96x on GroupNorm. Batching is only worth reaching for at N >= 4.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Two designs, one machine, one power mode, one sitting. The passthrough was run twice and
  both series are printed above; the N=2 row moved from 900.6 to 734.3 us between them and
  the N=1 row from 746.3 to 848.9. Read the trend across N, not single cells. GroupNorm was
  run once at 2 reps per size.
- The host share is a subtraction (wall/call minus the runlist bracket), not a profile of
  IRON's run(). It is therefore an upper bound on per-call host work: it includes the Python
  loop, buffer allocation inside the block, and anything else between the calls.
- Nothing here REDUCES the host share. Which part of IRON's per-call work dominates it --
  ABI validation, buffer preparation, instruction-buffer setup -- would need a profile of
  run() and was not done. That is the obvious next move and it is now worth more than any
  further dispatch work.
- The GroupNorm arm uses random inputs and checks that every output is finite and that more
  than half are non-zero. That detects a batch that silently did nothing, which is the
  failure mode batching introduces. It is NOT an accuracy check; groupnorm.py's own verifier
  covers that, and the 835.8 us figure this compares against came from a golden run.
- 617.0, 169.8, 447.3 and 36.3 us are quoted from earlier logs, not re-measured here. Only
  the tables above are from this sitting, and this sitting's own unbatched medians (676.2,
  729.0) sit above the published 617.0, so the ratios above use each series' own baseline.
- max_in_flight was set equal to the batch size, so each row is a single flush. Larger
  batches with a smaller in-flight window were not swept, and neither was the question of
  whether the batched runs execute concurrently or are serialised inside the driver.
- Verification is per batch, after the flush. A batch whose runs executed in the wrong ORDER
  would still pass, since each call writes its own buffers.
